>> title: Vindaloo Paste
>> category: Spice Blends & Marinades
>> tags: goan, chile, vinegar, paste, pork
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: vindalho, vindaloo masala, recheado-style paste, goan vindaloo paste
>> pairs-with: vindaloo
>> servings: 1 cup
>> time: 45 min
>> step.1: toast 2 min, until they smell of the tin
>> step.2: grind to a powder
>> step.3: soak 30 min in warm vinegar
>> step.4: blend 3 min to a smooth paste

Toast @cumin seeds{2%tsp}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp}, @cloves{8}, @brown mustard seeds{1/2%tsp}, @green cardamom{4%pods} and @cinnamon{1%stick}(2-in piece) in a dry #skillet{} ~toast{2%min}, shaking the pan, until they darken a shade and smell of the tin they came out of.

Grind @&(~1)toasted spices{} to a fine powder in a #spice grinder{}.

Soak @dried Kashmiri chiles{15}(stemmed and shaken out) and @dried árbol chiles{3}(stemmed; more if you mean it) in @palm vinegar{3/4%cup}(180 mL; cider vinegar is the usual stand-in), warmed, ~soak{30%min} until they are soft enough to tear.

Blend @&(~1)soaked chiles and their vinegar{} with @&(~2)ground spice{}, @garlic{12%cloves}(peeled), @fresh ginger{2%Tbs}(chopped), @jaggery{1%Tbs}(15 g; or dark brown sugar), @ground turmeric{1%tsp} and @kosher salt{2%tsp} in a #blender{} ~stir{3%min}, scraping down twice, until there is no grit left between your fingers.

Vinegar and garlic, not chile heat, are what vindaloo actually tastes of — the name is carne de vinha d'alhos, meat in wine and garlic, and Goa swapped the wine for palm vinegar and added the chiles. A cup is enough for 3 lb of pork, and it keeps a month under a film of oil.
